WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.260
Losing this channel removes pain.

2
00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.320
How could changing the same gene cause too much pain?

3
00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:12.050
Variants in the same gene run the other way.

4
00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:16.390
These ones alter gating, and their channels are expressed and conducting.

5
00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:21.650
In primary erythermalgia, people have burning pain in the hands and feet.

6
00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:24.330
The channel protein is there.

7
00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:26.870
Something about its gating has gone wrong.

8
00:00:27.883 --> 00:00:32.203
In gating terms, what would a sodium channel have to do wrong to cause too much

9
00:00:32.203 --> 00:00:33.203
pain?

10
00:00:38.517 --> 00:00:41.997
Three gating changes turn up in pain-associated variants.

11
00:00:42.937 --> 00:00:46.137
Open too easily, close too slowly, or fail to inactivate.

12
00:00:47.217 --> 00:00:49.977
All three have been found in people, in the same gene.

13
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:56.000
Yang and colleagues found missense variants in a family with erythermalgia, and in a

14
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:57.000
sporadic patient.

15
00:00:57.983 --> 00:01:00.843
The next step is the one this field insists on.

16
00:01:01.623 --> 00:01:03.543
Take each variant into a recording rig.

17
00:01:04.483 --> 00:01:08.863
The recordings were made at about room temperature, where sodium-channel kinetics

18
00:01:08.863 --> 00:01:09.863
are slower.

19
00:01:10.433 --> 00:01:15.193
Two of those variants were expressed in cultured cells and compared with wild type.

20
00:01:16.166 --> 00:01:19.366
Activation was shifted toward more negative voltages.

21
00:01:20.166 --> 00:01:21.926
The channel opened where it should be shut.

22
00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:24.420
Deactivation was slowed.

23
00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:26.940
Once open, the channel lingered.

24
00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:32.920
A channel that opens too easily and closes too late should make its neuron easier to

25
00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:37.940
drive. That is an expectation from a cell line, not a recording of the neuron.

26
00:01:38.933 --> 00:01:42.333
Paroxysmal extreme pain disorder is a third pattern.

27
00:01:43.353 --> 00:01:44.793
Fast inactivation is impaired.

28
00:01:45.816 --> 00:01:51.176
What is left behind is persistent sodium current, in three mutant channels tested in

29
00:01:51.176 --> 00:01:52.176
vitro.

30
00:01:52.516 --> 00:01:55.976
Same gene, three distinct channel lesions, three phenotypes.

31
00:01:57.016 --> 00:02:00.976
So one gene can remove pain in one family and cause it in another.

32
00:02:02.016 --> 00:02:05.956
These gating measurements came from channels expressed in cultured cells.

33
00:02:06.856 --> 00:02:10.356
The pain syndromes themselves were observed and reported in people.

34
00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:15.660
And a gating change still has to pass through the neuron before it reaches

35
00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:16.660
behaviour.

36
00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:19.770
So the gating step tells you what the protein does.

37
00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:23.330
What the neuron does with that is the next film's question.

38
00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:26.490
A variant list is not a mechanism.

39
00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:29.570
The gating step is where the mechanism starts.
